mcp/sdk: honour a complete schema definition on a parameter

`#[Schema(definition: [...])]` is documented as "the complete JSON schema array…
takes precedence over individual properties", and at the *method* level
`SchemaGenerator` unwraps it exactly so. At the *parameter* level it did not: the
attribute's `toArray()` was merged into the inferred schema as an ordinary key,
so the property came out as

```json
"track": {
    "type": "string",
    "default": "backend",
    "definition": { "type": "string", "x-mcp-header": "Track" }
}
```

A nested `definition` member is not JSON Schema. No client reads it, and
everything it holds is silently lost.

That matters more than it looks on the 2026-07-28 branch, because `x-mcp-header`
— the annotation that mirrors an argument into `Mcp-Param-*` so an intermediary
can route without parsing the body — has no representation on the `Schema`
attribute other than `definition`. Nesting it means the server never learns the
argument is header-mirrored, so it never validates the two against each other,
and the `-32020` the revision requires on a mismatch is never emitted.

The fix mirrors the method-level path: a `definition` replaces the property
schema. The inferred `default` is kept when the definition does not state one,
since that comes from the signature rather than from the schema.

Found by the demo's 2026-07-28 regression suite, whose "a header that disagrees
with the body is refused" check passed a contradictory `Mcp-Param-Track` and was
answered normally.

diff --git a/src/Capability/Discovery/SchemaGenerator.php b/src/Capability/Discovery/SchemaGenerator.php
index 3975a6a..9f745b4 100644
--- a/src/Capability/Discovery/SchemaGenerator.php
+++ b/src/Capability/Discovery/SchemaGenerator.php
@@ -237,7 +237,25 @@ final class SchemaGenerator implements SchemaGeneratorInterface
         // Parameter-level takes highest precedence
         $parameterLevelSchema = $paramInfo['parameter_schema'];
         if (!empty($parameterLevelSchema)) {
-            $mergedSchema = array_merge($mergedSchema, $parameterLevelSchema);
+            // A `definition` *is* the schema — the attribute documents it as taking
+            // precedence over the individual keywords, and the method-level path
+            // already unwraps it the same way. Merging it as an ordinary key would
+            // instead emit a property with a literal "definition" member, which no
+            // client reads and which silently drops whatever it holds — including
+            // `x-mcp-header`, the only way to express header mirroring from an
+            // attribute.
+            if (isset($parameterLevelSchema['definition']) && \is_array($parameterLevelSchema['definition'])) {
+                $mergedSchema = $parameterLevelSchema['definition'];
+
+                // The inferred default is still worth keeping: it comes from the
+                // signature, not from the schema, so a definition that does not
+                // mention it is not overriding it.
+                if (!\array_key_exists('default', $mergedSchema) && \array_key_exists('default', $inferredSchema)) {
+                    $mergedSchema['default'] = $inferredSchema['default'];
+                }
+            } else {
+                $mergedSchema = array_merge($mergedSchema, $parameterLevelSchema);
+            }
         }
 
